Found while fixing #172 (duplicate task ids). With the id counter fixed, a stress run of 8 parallel `karr create` in one clone still lost a whole card in 2 of 20 runs: 8 creates reported success, only 7 task refs survived, and the missing card was gone locally, not just on the remote.

Mechanism (not yet traced end to end, but the two halves are visible in the code):

  * every push uses the forced, pruning board refspec (+refs/karr/*:refs/karr/*, prune => 1), so a push resolves the local refs when it starts and then makes the remote identical to that snapshot. Two pushes overlapping in one clone means the one that STARTED earlier can LAND later and prune the newer card off the remote.
  * push then calls _mirror_local_state, so the mirror claims the pusher~s snapshot, and the next pull reads the pruned card as "L == T, R gone -- the remote deleted it" and deletes it locally too (_reconcile_with_mirror, the #49 branch). That branch is silent by design: nothing is parked, nothing is warned about.

Repro (same shape as #172, no real remote):
  git init --bare remote.git; git init work; cd work
  git config user.email t@e.st; git config user.name T
  git remote add origin ../remote.git
  echo x > README.md; git add -A; git commit -qm init
  karr init
  for i in $(seq 1 8); do karr create "Par$i" & done; wait
  git for-each-ref refs/karr/tasks | wc -l   # sometimes 7, with 8 "Created task" lines

Same run also produces per-ref push rejections from the bare remote ("failed to write reference refs/karr/tasks/N/data: a reference with that name already exists"), which fail the create AFTER the card was written locally -- the card is fine, the command exits non-zero. Worth understanding alongside this: both come from several pushes of the whole namespace being in flight at once.

Not a regression from #172; that fix only changed how refs/karr/meta/next-id is adopted.
Fixed in 944ddb2. Cause: the board push used a pruning refspec, which declares the pusher's local refs to be the whole namespace -- so a card another clone (or another process) had just created was deleted off the remote, _mirror_local_state made refs/karr-remote/ agree, and the owning clone's next pull read it as 'L == T, R gone' and deleted it locally too. No concurrency needed to see it: 'karr sync --push' from a clone that had not pulled destroyed the other clone's card deterministically (subtest 1 of t/178, red on HEAD).

Fix: a board push publishes only what the clone did -- its own refs plus one delete refspec per board ref it deleted itself, recorded as a tombstone under refs/karr-local/deleted/ when the ref goes, so a deletion whose push failed is still published by the next push (what prune achieved by accident). The mirror now follows the pre-push snapshot the refspec was built from plus the published deletions, never a post-push read of the board: lagging the remote is the direction reconciliation converges on, running ahead of it is the direction that loses cards.

Verification: t/178-push-does-not-prune-unseen-cards.t (3 subtests) -- red on HEAD, green with the fix. 12 stress runs of 8 parallel creates: 8 local and 8 remote task refs every time (was 2/20 losses). Full suite PASS: Files=164, Tests=1105.

